dlbk5dump recovers family-history data from Brother's Keeper 5 databases.
Build with GNU make and a C++17 compiler, then run from the repository root
with the legacy files in BK5/. Database files are read-only inputs.

    make
    ./dlbk5dump --gedcom > indexed.ged
    ./dlbk5dump --gedcom --clean > indexed-clean.ged

GEDCOM generation is implemented entirely in C++; Python is not required.
The default audit export preserves extraction notes, source addresses, raw
date slots, ALTKEYS slots and unassigned BKMAIL address-book text. --clean
omits extraction notes and the address-book inventory while retaining
genealogical notes, citations, event places and unresolved information.
Both modes produce GEDCOM 5.5.1 with UTF-8 text and a submitter record.

The Makefile provides the same exports:

    make gedcom                 # audit GEDCOM to stdout
    make gedcom-file            # indexed.ged
    make gedcom-clean-file      # indexed-clean.ged

File targets generate a temporary file and replace the old export only on
success. When capturing make's stdout, use make -s gedcom > indexed.ged:
otherwise compiler commands can appear there if a rebuild is needed. The
direct ./dlbk5dump command always sends only GEDCOM to stdout; diagnostics
go to stderr. --clean is accepted only after --gedcom.

The Python exporter tools/bk5-indexed-to-gedcom.py is retained as a reference.
The native exporter uses the same C++ person, family and lookup decoders
in process, and implements the reference's formatting and ordering rules.
To compare every output byte, including clean mode:

    make check-gedcom

This requires Python 3 for the reference and checks the bundled database,
CP437 text, missing/malformed inputs, and native export with no executables
on PATH. Comparison outputs and synthetic inputs use temporary directories;
the existing indexed.ged and BK5 files are not modified by this check.
Before changing export behavior, save the previous GEDCOM for comparison.

Extraction auditing is also native C++:

    make audit                      # export and audit, no Python required
    ./dlbk5dump --audit indexed.ged  # audit an existing extraction
    ./dlbk5dump --gedcom | ./dlbk5dump --audit
    make check-audit                # compare with the Python reference

--audit [GEDCOM] reads the named UTF-8 file or stdin when omitted, then
compares it with the current BK5 person dumps and mail inventory. Run from
the repository root with the same required DT5 files as the person decoder.
It uses the shared decoders in process; no subprocesses are started. Report
stdout and exit status match tools/bk5-audit.py on valid UTF-8 input.

Status 1 means missing families, dangling references, mismatched raw-date or
mail-note counts, an unresolved lookup, or an ALTKEYS note count other than
one. Duplicate IDs, ordinary date counts and undecodable-record counts are
reported but do not alone cause failure, preserving the reference policy.
The one-ALTKEYS-note requirement is specific to this database. Clean exports
lack the extraction notes required by this audit. Use the independent
validators below for GEDCOM structure and genealogy plausibility checks.

Native input errors return 1 with concise stderr and no partial report;
invalid arguments return 2. Native input must be valid UTF-8 for both files
and stdin, whereas the Python reference's stdin decoding depends on its
runtime environment. make check-audit covers every failure condition,
nonfatal counts, file/stdin input, Unicode and line endings, malformed or
missing inputs, and execution without other executables on PATH. Python
is needed only for comparison with the retained reference.

Ancestry inspection is native C++ and needs only the named GEDCOM file:

    ./dlbk5dump --ancestry indexed.ged --person 3
    ./dlbk5dump --ancestry indexed.ged --person 3 --terminals
    make check-ancestry

Person 3 is the default; person IDs are preserved as text, including synthetic
IDs such as R139096. Follow only explicit FAMC links. Tree output retains
unresolved people/families and suppresses revisits using one shared visited
set, including repeated ancestors reached by separate branches. Terminal
mode lists reachable people with no resolved parent family; an existing
family with no parents contributes no terminal person. Names and dates are
reported as stored, without inferring new relationships.

Keep tools/bk5-ancestry.py as the independent reference. make check-ancestry
compares both modes for every exported individual and fixtures for cycles,
missing links, duplicate records, Unicode and line endings, including native
execution without BK5 files or other executables on PATH. The shared reader
in src/gedcom_reader.cpp preserves input order and CR/LF/CRLF handling.
Input errors return 1; absent people and invalid arguments return 2. Native
diagnostics/help use the native command name; option names must be complete
(Python argparse also accepts unambiguous abbreviations). No Python or
subprocesses are used during native ancestry inspection.

Independent validation (Python 3 required):

    ./tools/gedcom-check.py indexed.ged
    ./tools/gedcom-roles.py indexed.ged
    ./tools/gedcom-places.py indexed.ged

The export remains an intermediate extraction: raw-only records appear
under synthetic @IR...@ identifiers, and ambiguous dates or references are
retained as labelled notes. No missing person or relationship is invented.
The ancestry tool follows explicit FAMC links only.

Useful decoder commands:

    ./dlbk5dump --dump-altkeys
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-options
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-mail
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-id-records
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-records
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-families
    ./dlbk5dump --trace-family 31
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-sources
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-locations
    ./dlbk5dump --dump-marriages
    ./dlbk5dump --family 0x9a

--dump-altkeys replaces tools/bk5-altkeys.py for normal use. It needs only
BK5/ALTKEYS.DT5 and runs entirely in C++. It checks that the file consists
of complete 162-byte slots, decodes CP437 text before the first '<', and
reports non-empty slots with their original zero-based slot numbers. Blank
slots contribute to the summary count; slot numbers are not person IDs.
The GEDCOM exporter uses the same ALTKEYS reader. For this database:

    ALTKEYS_SLOT slot=16 text='Bournemouth, Dorset'
    ALTKEYS_SLOTS total=19 nonempty=1

The Python script remains an independent reference. Run make check-altkeys
to compare outputs byte for byte on the bundled file and temporary fixtures
covering every CP437 byte, control/quote escaping, whitespace, full and empty
slots, and invalid or missing input. The check also verifies that the native
flag works without other database files or executables on PATH.

--dump-options replaces tools/bk5-options.py for normal use. It compares
BK5/BKOPTION.DT5 with BK5/BKOPTION.BAK using only those two files and runs
entirely in C++. Records are separated by CR, LF or CRLF, decoded as CP437,
and stripped of trailing whitespace. Each record containing '=' is split at
the first '='; leading spaces and spaces in keys are preserved. Duplicate
keys keep the last value. Differences are printed in Unicode key order,
using <missing> for absent keys, followed by counts. For this database:

    BKOPTION_SUMMARY active_keys=103 backup_keys=103 differences=8

A completed comparison exits successfully even when differences exist.
Missing/unreadable input returns status 1; invalid arguments return 2.
These are configuration differences, not genealogy records.

The Python script remains an independent reference. Run make check-options
to compare output on the bundled files and temporary fixtures covering
line endings, whitespace, duplicate and empty keys, missing values, CP437
text and Unicode sorting, empty files, missing inputs and invalid arguments.
The check also runs the native flag without other database files or
executables on PATH.

--dump-mail replaces tools/bk5-mail.py for normal use. It needs only
BK5/BKMAIL.DT5 and runs entirely in C++. Runs contain bytes 0x20-0x7e;
all control, DEL and high bytes delimit runs. Spaces are trimmed, and only
text of at least three characters is reported. Runs ending at EOF are kept.
Quoting and report stdout match the Python reference byte for byte:

    BKMAIL_TEXT_RUNS count=43

Preserve the reference's offset convention: --dump-mail labels the offset
immediately after the raw run, before trimming, which is its delimiter or
EOF. GEDCOM audit notes instead label the raw run's start. The shared reader
retains both boundaries so existing GEDCOM output is unchanged. All mail
text stays unassigned; a similar name does not establish person ownership.

Run make check-mail to compare reports and verify input bytes are unchanged.
Fixtures cover every byte value, raw offsets, spaces, quote/backslash
escaping, short and long runs, empty/binary-only files and EOF. The check
also verifies execution with only BKMAIL.DT5 and no executables on PATH.
Missing/unreadable input returns 1 with no report; native errors use concise
stderr messages rather than Python tracebacks. Extra arguments return 2.
The Python script remains an independent reference. make check-gedcom also
compares synthetic mail fixtures in audit and clean mode, including raw
start offsets and long-note wrapping.

The person decoder reads the key-0 index, then scans physical fixed slots
for valid person numbers missing from the index. Raw-page scans preserve a
second view of the variable data, including old or orphaned records.

Family membership comes from the 167 decoded BKMARR.DT5 family records,
including spouse and child IDs. Person references not confirmed by that
table remain notes. Marriage dates, places, source citations, ceremony
status and documented divorce/annulment/separation codes are exported.
--dump-marriages is an older diagnostic date scan; --dump-families is the
record-aware family decoder. --dump-relations reports unvalidated B-tree
pointer candidates and must not be used as genealogy relationships.

Person date/location slots are birth, baptism, death, burial and other.
Slots 1-4 produce matching events with dates and/or places; slot 5 and raw
dates of unproven type remain notes. BKSOURCE descriptions become standalone
SOUR records. Person citations, with PAGE and QUAY, attach to the matching
event when exported, otherwise to the person; family citations stay on the
family. All five person source/location slot notes remain in audit mode.

ALTKEYS and BKMAIL text stays unassigned unless ownership is independently
proven. BKOPTION and its backup are configuration, not genealogy data.
